#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7da551-fc32-5399-967a-ca52f4de957a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292091} {:answer/id #uuid "d11c9e26-adff-5a42-bbcb-d4dc4a59cb34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292092} {:answer/id #uuid "91b18fc2-d4f8-576c-95ba-ab19794784eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+3=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292093} {:answer/id #uuid "4a538e83-fa76-5561-b11d-5d460fa79700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-4t,\\,\\,y-1=-2{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292094} {:answer/id #uuid "00fe46db-a3d4-5b1b-a9b8-b17856c473cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00fe46db-a3d4-5b1b-a9b8-b17856c473cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-1=-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292095} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6b11901-df19-53f7-b179-442583776fda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "914c8844-0878-5387-a79a-b5ca32d2ccb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-106462" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(3,1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,-7).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(3,1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-3=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=-7$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-8=p{t}^{2} \\\\ -8=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =2pt \\\\ -4 & =pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{4}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8 & =p{(-\\frac{4}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -8 & =p\\cdot \\frac{16}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -8 & =\\frac{16}{p} \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-3=(-2){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-2)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-4t.\n$$"}]}
